- How would you modify to also return the actual blocks boundaries when multiple optimal partitions exist?
- How does the solution change if limit is dynamic per block?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess understanding of Trie data structure and practical prefix search used in autocompletion; tests familiarity with basic data structures and iteration over strings.

## Key Concepts
- Trie structure with 26-way children
- insert/search/startsWith time complexity O(m)
- memory proportional to number of unique prefixes

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode { constructor(){ this.next = new Array(26).fill(null); this.end=false; } }
class Trie { constructor(){ this.root = new TrieNode(); }
 insert(word){ let p=this.root; for(let ch of word){ let idx=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[idx]) p.next[idx]=new TrieNode(); p=p.next[idx]; } p.end=true; }
 search(word){ let p=this.root; for(let ch of word){ let idx=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[idx]) return false; p=p.next[idx]; } return p.end; }
 startsWith(prefix){ let p=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ let idx=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[idx]) return false; p=p.next[idx]; } return true; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for memory?
- How would you extend to support deletion or word counts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a DP over partitions using prefix sums and a hashmap to achieve linear-time partition counting. This also exercises both bottom-up and top-down reasoning, and edge-case handling for K and negative numbers.

## Key Concepts

- Prefix sums
- Hash map DP
- Edge-case handling (K=0, negative numbers)

## Code Example

```javascript
function countPartitions(A, K) {
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const N = A.length;
  const prefix = new Array(N+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= N; i++) prefix[i] = prefix[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = new Array(N+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  const map = new Map();
  map.set(0, 1);
  for (let i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
    const key = prefix[i] - K;
    const ways = map.get(key) ?? 0;
    dp[i] = ways;
    const cur = (map.get(prefix[i]) ?? 0) + dp[i];
    map.set(prefix[i], cur % MOD);
  }
  return dp[N] % MOD;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to count partitions with an additional constraint, e.g., every block length <= L?
- What if A contains large numbers and K is dynamic per query?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model DP with obstacles and variable step sizes, requiring careful base cases and transition logic. It also assesses both memoization and tabulation familiarity.

## Key Concepts

- DP with multiple transitions (1..k)
- Handling blocked steps
- Memoization vs tabulation
- Time complexity: O(n*maxJump); Space: O(n) (or O(maxJump) with optimization)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Bottom-up tabulation
function countWays(n, maxJump, blocked) {
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    if (blocked[i]) { dp[i] = 0; continue; }
    for (let j = 1; j <= maxJump; j++) {
      if (i - j >= 0) dp[i] += dp[i - j];
    }
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

```javascript
// Memoized recursive version
function countWaysRec(n, maxJump, blocked, i = n, memo = {}) {
  if (i === 0) return 1;
  if (i < 0 || blocked[i]) return 0;
  if (memo[i] !== undefined) return memo[i];
  let ways = 0;
  for (let t = 1; t <= maxJump; t++) ways += countWaysRec(n, maxJump, blocked, i - t, memo);
  memo[i] = ways;
  return ways;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(maxJump) space with a sliding window?
- How would you adjust for modulo arithmetic to prevent overflow for large n?

