## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates dynamic programming skills with state expansion, requiring candidates to track whether the cell removal has been used. It tests proper handling of grid boundary conditions and provides opportunities to discuss space optimization strategies and compare memoization versus tabulation approaches.

## Key Concepts
- DP with auxiliary state tracking
- Space optimization using rolling arrays
- Boundary condition handling
- State transition logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPathWithOneRemoval(grid) {
  const m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
  const dp0 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(Infinity));
  const dp1 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(Infinity));

  dp0[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp1[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (i > 0) {
        dp0[i][j] = Math.min(dp0[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp0[i-1][j]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp1[i-1][j]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], dp0[i-1][j]);
      }
      if (j > 0) {
        dp0[i][j] = Math.min(dp0[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp0[i][j-1]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp1[i][j-1]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], dp0[i][j-1]);
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.min(dp0[m-1][n-1], dp1[m-1][n-1]);
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity
- Time: O(m × n) - we process each cell once
- Space: O(m × n) - can be optimized to O(n) using rolling arrays since we only need the previous row

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical DP reasoning: variable step costs require deriving both a memoized and a tabular solution, handle unreachable states, and discuss trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Top-down DP with memoization
- Bottom-up DP (tabulation)
- Handling unreachable states with Infinity
- Time/space complexity: O(n|S|) time, O(n) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostTopDown(n, steps, memo=new Map()){
  if(n===0) return 0
  if(n<0) return Infinity
  if(memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n)
  let best = Infinity
  for(let s of steps){
    const c = s + minCostTopDown(n-s, steps, memo)
    if(c < best) best = c
  }
  memo.set(n, best)
  return best
}

function minCostBottomUp(n, steps){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill(INF)
  dp[0] = 0
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let s of steps){
      if(i>=s && dp[i-s] !== INF){
        dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], s + dp[i-s])
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n] === INF ? -1 : dp[n]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if costs were a non-linear function cost(s) instead of s? 
- How would you extend to count the number of ways to reach n with min cost, not just the cost itself?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to convert dependency graphs into scheduling via topological order; checks understanding of longest-path DP on DAG, cycle detection, and clear complexity analysis.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort (Kahn / DFS)
- DAG longest path DP
- Cycle detection
- Complexity analysis

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestFinishTimes(n, edges, durations) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  const finish = durations.slice();
  let seen = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    seen++;
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      finish[v] = Math.max(finish[v], finish[u] + durations[v]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  if (seen !== n) throw new Error('Cycle detected');
  return finish;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for multiple start times or optional prerequisites?
- How does the solution change if edge weights represent communication delay instead of task duration?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes understanding of iterative approaches, partition logic, and complexity trade-offs between quicksort and mergesort. 

## Key Concepts
- Iterative quicksort with explicit stack
- Hoare partition scheme and pivot strategy
- Time/space complexity comparisons with mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
function iterativeQuicksort(a){
  const n=a.length;
  const stack=[[0,n-1]];
  const swap=(i,j)=>{let t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  while(stack.length){
    const [l,r]=stack.pop();
    if(l>=r) continue;
    let pivot=a[(l+r)>>1];
    let i=l, j=r;
    while(i<=j){
      while(a[i]<pivot) i++;
      while(a[j]>pivot) j--;
      if(i<=j){ swap(i,j); i++; j--; }
    }
    if(l<j) stack.push([l,j]);
    if(i<r) stack.push([i,r]);
  }
  return a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to guarantee O(n log n) worst-case?
- Compare stability with mergesort; when would you choose one over the other?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to combine topological sorting with dynamic programming for shortest paths, avoiding Dijkstra's heap in DAGs. Uses O(n+m) time and O(n+m) memory and handles unreachable nodes.

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort
- Relaxation in topo order
- DAG shortest-path correctness
- Edge cases: disconnected nodes, zero-weight edges

## Code Example

```javascript
function dagShortestPaths(n, edges, s){
  const adj = Array.from({length:n},()=>[]);
  for (const [u,v,w] of edges){ adj[u].push([v,w]); }
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){ indeg[v]++; }
  const q=[];
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(indeg[i]===0) q.push(i);
  const topo=[];
  for(let i=0;i<q.length;i++){
    const u=q[i];
    topo.push(u);
    for(const [v,w] of adj[u]){
      if(--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const INF=1e15;
  const dist=Array(n).fill(INF);
  dist[s]=0;
  for(const u of topo){
    if(dist[u]===INF) continue;
    for(const [v,w] of adj[u]){
      dist[v]=Math.min(dist[v], dist[u]+w);
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify for multiple sources?
- How to handle negative weights if cycles are absent? (not in DAG)

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests fundamental graph algorithms, DAG analysis, and dynamic programming techniques commonly used in project scheduling and dependency management systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Topological Sorting**: Linear ordering respecting task dependencies
- **Dynamic Programming on DAGs**: Computing optimal values through predecessor relationships
- **Earliest Finish Times**: Maximum of predecessor completion plus task duration
- **Makespan Calculation**: Overall project completion time
- **Complexity**: O(N + E) time and space

## Code Example
```javascript
function makespan(n, durations, edges) {
  // Build adjacency list and compute indegrees
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  // Initialize earliest finish times
  const earliestFinish = durations.slice();
  const queue = [];

  // Start with source nodes (indegree = 0)
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) queue.push(i);
  }

  // Process nodes in topological order
  let head = 0;
  while (head < queue.length) {
    const u = queue[head++];

    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      // Update earliest finish for successor
      earliestFinish[v] = Math.max(
        earliestFinish[v],
        earliestFinish[u] + durations[v]
      );

      indeg[v]--;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) queue.push(v);
    }
  }

  // Return makespan (maximum earliest finish time)
  return Math.max(...earliestFinish);
}
```

## Algorithm Insight
The topological approach ensures each node is processed only after all its predecessors are complete, allowing accurate computation of earliest start times and efficient makespan calculation.

